{
    "answer": "PHH stands for Priority Households. It is one of the beneficiary categories under the National Food Security Act (NFSA) that receives subsidised foodgrains (along with AAY, covering 75% of the rural and 50% of the urban population). Each person under a PHH ration card is entitled to 5 kg of foodgrains per month, which is provided free of cost under PMGKAY since January 1, 2023. You can also use a PHH ration card across different states and union territories under the ONORC scheme.",
    "confidence": "high",
    "follow_up": "Would you like to know the difference between PHH and AAY ration cards?"
}

{
    "answer": "Antyodaya Anna Yojana (AAY) is a government scheme launched in December 2000 to support the poorest of the poor families under the Targeted Public Distribution System (TPDS). Under this scheme, eligible AAY families receive 35 kg of foodgrains per family per month at highly subsidized rates (Rs. 2/kg for wheat and Rs. 3/kg for rice), and are also covered for free-of-cost foodgrains under PMGKAY. State and UT governments identify these families—such as landless laborers, daily wage workers, households headed by widows, disabled persons, or the elderly with no support, and primitive tribal households—and issue them distinctive AAY ration cards.",
    "confidence": "high",
    "follow_up": "Would you like to know the detailed eligibility criteria for getting an AAY ration card?"
}
